    "rationale": "A is the correct answer because the federal district court has subject matter jurisdiction over this class action. In class actions filed in federal court, diversity jurisdiction is based on the named representative\u2019s citizenship. In general, the named class representatives must be diverse from the defendants. See Supreme Tribe of Ben-Hur v. Cauble, 255 U.S. 356 (1921). This is the approach to diversity jurisdiction applicable to class actions filed under Rule 23. However, pursuant to the Class Action Fairness Act (28 U.S.C. 1332(d)(2)), the district courts have original jurisdiction over any civil action where the amount in controversy exceeds the sum of $5,000,000 and the action is a class action where any member of the class is a citizen of a state different from the defendant. This is called \u201cminimal diversity.\u201d Here, the defendant Pharma Co. is a citizen of State A (incorporation) and State B (principal place of business). The class representative is also a citizen of State B but since there are class members from State C and the amount in controversy exceeds $5,000,000, the court has proper subject matter jurisdiction over this class action under 1332(d)(2). Therefore, Answer A is correct and Answer C is incorrect. Answer B is incorrect because subject matter jurisdiction is not a matter of plaintiff\u2019s choice but a matter of jurisdiction. While the plaintiff has the right to choose initially which court will adjudicate their claims, the court must have proper jurisdiction in order to do so. Answer D is incorrect because under 28 U.S.C. 1332(d)(2), the district courts have original jurisdiction over any civil action where the amount in controversy exceeds the sum of $5,000,000 and the action is a class action where any member of the class is a citizen of a state different from the defendant. Here, we have a class action and the sum exceeds $5,000,000.",
