    "rationale": "B is the correct answer. Pursuant to the Federal Rules of Appellate Procedure (FRAP) 4, an appeal is permitted only from a final judgment. See 28 U.S.C \u00a71291. Interlocutory orders are generally not immediately appealable. There are exceptions to the \u201cfinal judgment rule\u201d under 28 U.S.C. \u00a71292(a)(1), allowing for interlocutory orders. For example, an order granting, continuing, modifying, refusing ,or dissolving injunctions, or refusing to dissolve or modify injunctions or where there is a controlling question of law, under 28 U.S.C. \u00a71292(b). However, none of the exceptions to the final judgment rule apply here. Therefore, the Defendant\u2019s right to an appeal has not yet vested. Answer A is incorrect because the Defendant\u2019s right to an appeal has not vested regardless of when the trial court\u2019s order compelling discovery expires. Answer C is incorrect because orders compelling disclosure of documents arguably protected by attorney-client privilege are not immediately appealable. See Mohawk Industries, Inc. v. Carpenter, 558 U.S. 100 (2009), where the Court held that \u201cdisclosure of orders adverse to the attorney-client privilege do not qualify for immediate appeal under the collateral order doctrine.\u201d Answer D is incorrect because this is not relevant to the issue of interlocutory appeals and the final judgment rule.",
