    "rationale": "B is the correct answer. Under the doctrine of claim preclusion\u2014res judicata\u2014claims that have been litigated fully, fairly, and with finality cannot be re-litigated. The doctrine precludes claims that were adjudicated and claims that should have been asserted in the original action. The Second Restatement of Judgments suggests that claims are barred if they arise out of the same group of operative facts or arise out of the same underlying transaction or occurrence. Compulsory counterclaims under FRCP 13(a) are counterclaims that arise from the same set of operative facts as the main claim asserted in court (here the loose floorboard, fall, and broken vase). Compulsory counterclaims are required to be asserted with the main claim or else are waived under claim preclusion. The Homeowner\u2019s allegation that the Guest was at fault and destroyed the vase is a claim that should have been asserted in the original action filed by the Guest. Since Homeowner failed to assert this claim, it is now barred from litigation in this subsequent action. Answer A is incorrect because, whether the Homeowner should have fixed the floorboard is not relevant to the determination as to claim preclusion. Answer C is incorrect because, while litigants generally have a right to a jury trial on all legal matters, this claim is barred by res judicata and cannot be re-litigated with or without a jury. Answer D is incorrect because a motion to dismiss under FRCP 12(b) and specifically 12(b)(6) is not the appropriate device for dismissal based on res judicata. A motion to dismiss under FRCP 12(b)(6) for failure to state a claim upon which relief can be granted looks to the four corners of the complaint to determine whether the Plaintiff has pled a plausible claim for relief. Unless the complaint itself makes clear that the subsequent action is barred by res judicata, the court cannot grant the motion to dismiss on res judicata grounds. Instead, res judicata is an affirmative defense raised in the pleadings.",
