    "id": "q16",
    "topic": "Collapse all questions",
    "questionText": "Homeowner and Contractor entered into a remodeling contract whereby Contractor agreed to remodel Homeowner\u2019s primary residence during the summer months while Homeowner stayed in her vacation home. According to the contract, the remodeling was to be completed by September 1, when Homeowner\u2019s children were set to return to school after summer vacation. Due to a delay in the delivery of construction materials, the project was not completed until mid-October. Homeowner filed a breach of contract action against Contractor in federal district court in State A under diversity jurisdiction. After the suit was filed, and based on his two subcontracts, Contractor filed a third-party complaint against Lumber Co. alleging that the breach of contract was caused by Lumber Co.\u2019s delay in delivering construction materials. Contractor also filed a third-party complaint against Fence, Inc. alleging that the breach of contract was also caused by Fence, Inc.\u2019s delay in delivering construction materials. Fence, Inc. then filed a cross-claim against Lumber Co. alleging that Lumber\u2019s delay in delivering the lumber caused Fence, Inc.\u2019s inability to provide the fencing under their contract with Contractor. Lumber Co. filed a motion to dismiss Fence, Inc.\u2019s cross-claim. How should the court rule on the motion?",
