    "correctAnswerIndex": 0,
    "rationale": "A is the correct answer. Under FRCP 14, a defending party may, as third-party plaintiff, serve a summons and complaint on a nonparty who is or may be liable to it for all or part of the claim against it. The non-party is called the third-party defendant. In turn, the third-party defendant may take any action that a defendant can take, i.e., default, file an answer, file a counterclaim, etc. FRCP 14 authorizes the third-party defendant to \u201cassert any counterclaim against the third-party plaintiff under Rule 13(a), and may assert any counterclaim against the third-party plaintiff under Rule 13(b) or any cross-claim against another third-party defendant under Rule 13(g)\" (emphasis added). See FRCP 14(a)(2)(B). Therefore, A is correct, and Answers C and D are incorrect. B is incorrect because judicial economy cannot justify joinder if the rules/statutes do not permit joinder or if the rules/statutes prohibit joinder. Here, FRCP 14 permits this cross-claim by one third-party defendant against another third-party defendant\u2014and judicial economy is achieved.",
