    "rationale": "B is the correct answer. When a contract contains an arbitration clause, federal courts do not have jurisdiction over the suit and a motion to dismiss for improper venue under FRCP 12(b)(3) is the proper motion to enforce the arbitration clause. See GHH, Inc. v. Apex Operating Inc., 434 F. Supp. 3d 645 (2020), citing Faulkenberg v. CB Tax Franchise Sys., LP, 637 F.3d. 801, 807 (7th Cir. 2011). The federal district court is the improper venue for an action where arbitration provision compels parties to arbitrate disputes. FRCP 12(b)(3). For this reason, Answers C and D are incorrect. Answer A is incorrect because the federal court would have subject matter jurisdiction over the third-party complaint (Defendant v. Switch Co.) under supplemental jurisdiction 28 U.S.C. \u00a71367(a) where \u201cthe district courts shall have supplemental jurisdiction over all other claims that are so related to claims in the action within such original jurisdiction that they form part of the same case or controversy.\u201d The prohibition in 28 U.S.C. \u00a71367(b) relates to claims \u201cby plaintiffs against persons made parties under Rule 14[ . . . ].\u201d The third-party complaint is not a claim by the plaintiff and thus \u00a71367(b) does not apply.",
